  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nUpper troughing continues to move off into the Great Lakes,\nleaving the Northern Plains under northwesterly flow aloft\ntonight with surface high pressure. With decreasing clouds \ntonight and light winds, can't completely rule out some patchy \nground fog although the HREF probabilities of visibility less \nthan 1 mile is minimal. Upper ridging builds back into the \nWestern Plains late tomorrow, with most models agreeing on a \nweak shortwave coming over top of the ridge on Friday. Mean CAPE\nvalues look fairly unimpressive Friday except in our far \nsouthwestern counties. A few of the machine learning solutions \nhave some probability of severe impacts in portions of our west \non Friday, but some tend to run a bit more bullish than reality,\nso will hold off messaging but continue to monitor. \n\n\nWith the upper ridge coming down and southwesterly flow aloft \nsetting up until a trough comes through the international border\nregion Monday, it looks like there will be an active pattern \ninto the weekend. At least some instability according to the NBM\nmean CAPE, but spread among the members is almost as much as \nthe mean values, 500-1000 J/kg. Predictability is too low to \nmessage any severe impacts at this point but chances look pretty\ngood for showers and thunderstorms given the forcing and time \nof year for Friday and the weekend. Ensemble means and clusters\nhave mostly southwesterly flow aloft into next week, so expect \nwarmer than average temperatures and periodic precip chances to \ncontinue.",
